+# EVIDENCE #2557 — ARW "SUPREMEAI_BACKEND_URL not configured": env-name-level proof from the render-mcp key list (zero-credential)
+
+- **Date**: 2026-10-30 (probes 07:10–07:15Z / 15:10–15:15 +08)
+- **Agent**: contributor-agent, Job 478318 (cron-agent-loop-202610301510), MCP session 632687c2
+- **Targets**: #2557 (audit:finding, P2-minor) · #3027 addendum (earlier mechanistic pass, 03:06Z)
+- **Tools**: `render_get_env_var_keys`, `render_service_health`, `render_list_services`, `agent_review_workflow_status` — zero-credential, R70 floor
+- **Claim**: GRANT_TAKEOVER on #2557 at 07:10:54Z (prior claim expired 02:08Z); report-only; no Closes line.
+
+## 1. Headline
+
+The ARW outage is now proven at the **server-side env-name level**: the render-mcp (Control Tower) service carries **42 env vars, and none of them is `SUPREMEAI_BACKEND_URL`** — no key with the substring `BACKEND` exists at all. The outage is therefore not a value error, not a read-path bug inside a running container, and not the primary/worker boot-kill family: the key the agent-review workflow requires was **never set (or was removed) on this service**. The remedy collapses to one admin action: add the key on render-mcp (or align the code to one of the three backend-URL keys that DO exist) — the service redeploys automatically.
+
+## 2. Evidence (verbatim, 07:10–07:15Z)
+
+| Probe | Result |
+| --- | --- |
+| `render_get_env_var_keys` render-mcp `srv-dabm7inqj5pc738jkrt0` | **42 keys**; `SUPREMEAI_BACKEND_URL` absent; substring-BACKEND matches: **none** |
+| Backend-URL-shaped keys that DO exist | `SUPREMEAI_GATEWAY_URL`, `SUPREMEAI_API_URL`, `RENDER_PRIMARY_URL` (+ `RENDER_MCP_URL`, `RENDER_PRIMARY_SVC_ID`, `RENDER_MCP_SVC_ID`) |
+| `agent_review_workflow_status` | `Error: SUPREMEAI_BACKEND_URL is not configured` (07:12Z — outage live) |
+| `render_service_health` render-mcp | `lastDeployStatus: live`, branch main, `suspended: not_suspended` |
+| `resources_dashboard` | render-mcp status `healthy` (contrast: primary/worker `degraded` per #3057 chain) |
+
+## 3. Why this is incremental over #3027
+
+- #3027 (03:06Z) proved the outage live and mapped its **mechanics** (dedup key is prompt-only; phantom self-clear; lock-scope). It inferred the cause from the tool's error string.
+- This pass proves the cause **structurally from the server side**: the env key list is name-complete and the required name is missing — eliminating the alternative hypotheses (value typo, partial rollout, code reading the wrong attribute of a present key). It also shows the tower service itself is healthy and deploy-live, so the fix is pure config, not a code deploy.
+- Remedy paths for the admin (pick one):
+  1. **Add `SUPREMEAI_BACKEND_URL`** on render-mcp, value = the core-api backend URL (the existing `RENDER_PRIMARY_URL` / `SUPREMEAI_API_URL` values are the natural candidates; values are masked on this plane so the correct one is an admin-side read).
+  2. **Or** change the agent-review workflow code to read one of the three keys that already exist — but that is a tower-code change (satellite), so path 1 is the smaller, config-only move.
+- After either: no manual redeploy needed (`autoDeployTrigger: commit`; env changes apply on the next deploy — admin may trigger one from the dashboard or the governed `action_render_deploy` HITL lane).
+
+## 4. Correlation with the #3057 boot-kill chain
+
+Deliberately **not** conflated: render-mcp is a different service, `deploy live`, `healthy` — the primary/worker boot-crash loop does not explain this outage. The two incidents share only the family trait "zero-credential health probes look green while a config/deploy defect persists".
